// @check
// @error `indexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `lastIndexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `includes` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `count` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `indexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over {[string]: i32} has no lowering
// @error `indexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over F[] has no lowering
// @error `indexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: (N | null)[] isn't equatable (a field is not value-comparable) — compare a projection whose components are value-comparable, and only one that no two distinct values share
// @error `indexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over (N | null)[] has no lowering
// @error `myIndexOf` compares its type parameter with `==` here: `==` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `==` over Circle[] has no lowering
// THE EQUATABILITY DIRECTIVE ABOVE RUNS TO THE END OF THE REMEDY CLAUSE ON PURPOSE, and it is
// the only place in the corpus that does. `@error` matches a SUBSTRING, so a directive that
// stops at `(a field is not value-comparable)` gives the clause after it ZERO coverage — and
// that is not hypothetical: the D46 close replaced the clause with a sentence that had no
// predicate (`— compare a projection whose components are`) and every gate stayed green. One
// directive carrying the whole sentence is the cheapest thing that would have caught it.
//
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D35. A `needle: T` the checker will not `==` used
// to LOSE that refusal in the instantiation, and the loss was uniform while its CONSEQUENCE
// was decided by luck one layer down.
//
// THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES IT SHARP, and it is the last function in this file: the same
// comparison written DIRECTLY has always been a loud checker error. So the compiler was never
// missing a lowering it might one day grow — it HAD the refusal, and the refusal did not
// survive the trip through a type parameter. One decision, two severities, chosen by the
// spelling. The control's message is the generic routes' message with the attribution clause
// removed, and that is deliberate: `eqRefusals` is ONE home, so the two spellings cannot drift
// into two sentences.
//
// WHERE IT WAS LOST. `checkBinary` asks two questions of the operands — `isEquatable` (a plain
// object/array compares field-by-field, which needs every component value-comparable) and
// `eqCmpKindOfTy` (does a compare CORE exist for this rep). Inside a generic body the operand
// is `T`, a `TyVar`: `isEquatable` answers TRUE and `eqCmpKindOfTy` answers "" (OPAQUE). Both
// answers are correct about a type variable and useless about the instance, and nothing
// re-asked once the pin was known. Both now ride the PIN — `binOpDefinedFor` and
// `validateBinCstrs` put the same two questions at the CALL SITE under the substituted type,
// which is the only place the concrete type and the refusal are both in scope, and it is a
// `vl check` answer rather than an engine one.
//
// ALL FOUR `needle: T` EXPORTS ARE HERE, and the reason to spell them out rather than trust
// one is that the sentence they replace did not: a draft of this file ran `indexOf` on four
// ROUTES and its header called that "all four exports". A count without its axis, which is the
// mistake `std/array.vl`'s own ledger catalogues, made while retiring an entry for it. Caught
// by the `std-api-reviewer` pass over this change — the fifth consecutive time that review has
// produced the closing change's next correction.
//
// THE AXIS IS EQUATABILITY OF `T`, NOT "A LIST WHOSE ELEMENT IS A STRUCT" — the row was filed
// one axis too narrow and the same review corrected it. `{[string]: i32}` has no struct in it
// and reproduced identically, which is the `CM` row below; `reverse` over the same `CM[]`
// receiver ran and still runs, so the receiver was never the question.
//
// **THE FULL-CLAUSE DIRECTIVE HAS MOVED TWICE AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE STILL HAS TO BE TRUE.**
// The `K[]` row carried it until 2026-08-26; D45 made that row RUN, and the first draft of
// that graduation deleted the row without moving the clause — leaving the paragraph above
// advertising a protection the corpus no longer had. `grep -rn "isn't equatable" tests/` then
// returned ONE file, whose directive is the bare substring and whose own comment says it
// "matches the refusal, not the remedy". Caught by the `std-api-reviewer` pass over that
// change, which is what this paragraph is for. It moved to `(K | null)[]`, and D101's close
// made THAT row run too — so it now sits on `(N | null)[]`, which is the same move for the
// same reason and is why the carrier is chosen for its REP rather than its spelling.
//
// WHY `(N | null)[]` IS THE RIGHT CARRIER, AND WHY IT IS NOT MERELY THE NEXT ONE FREE. It is
// the closest shape to the row that just graduated: `type N = 1 | 2` is a literal union like
// `K`, and `N | null` is spelled identically to `K | null` — but a NUMERIC litunion's nullable
// is a value-union BOX, not the i32 atom-sentinel niche the string one gets
// (`repOfNullable` asks `tyIsLitUnion`, which is string-only; `nulNumLitUnionBaseName`'s
// header says the same thing from the other side). So the two adjacent rows in this file now
// state D101's BOUNDARY: one spelling of `<litunion> | null` compares, its numeric twin does
// not, and the discriminator is the rep and not the syntax. Re-measured at this close rather
// than carried over — it is the shape that still prints BOTH sentences.
//
// THE `K[]` AND `(K | null)[]` ROWS BOTH GRADUATED; THE REPLACEMENTS ARE `F[]` AND
// `(N | null)[]`. The `(K | null)[]` cell RUNS as of D101 and its values are pinned in
// `tests/cases/literal-unions/nullable-litunion-list-equality.vl` (the compiler-side pin) and
// in `tests/cases/std/array-litunion-list-needle.vl` (all four `needle: T` exports).
// Until 2026-08-26 a `K[]` (`type K = "a" | "b"`) row stood here with **TWO** directives —
// the structural gate AND the missing list core — and the first of them was FACTUALLY WRONG
// about the shape: a literal union reps as ONE cell (the interned i32 atom for a string union,
// the base scalar for a numeric one), so `K == K` ran and printed the right answer while the
// list of them was refused for having "a field that is not value-comparable". That is
// `silent-class-inventory` D45, and it is closed; the cell RUNS and its value is pinned in
// `tests/cases/std/array-litunion-list-needle.vl` (the graduation, all four exports).
//
// `F[]` (`type F = 1.5 | 2.5`) trips ONE gate rather than two: an
// `f64`-based literal union rides the f64 list, whose `==` has no core at all — `f64[] ==
// f64[]` is refused written directly, which is the symmetry this file's control demonstrates
// for `Circle[]`. So the row that remains says only "no lowering", and a table that files
// every literal union under one bucket is wrong about THAT, which is the same mistake in the
// other direction.
//
// THE `K[]` ROW WAS ALSO THE ONE THAT USED TO RUN under D35, returning the correct index.
// What was lost then was uniformly the REFUSAL; whether a given `T` then produced invalid
// wasm or a working answer was decided DOWNSTREAM, by whether the emitter happens to hold a
// comparison for that rep. The D45 close is that observation acted on: the refusal was wrong
// for this rep and the emitter's list core was there all along.
import { count, includes, indexOf, lastIndexOf } from "std:array"

type Circle = { r: i32 }
type CM = {[string]: i32}
type F = 1.5 | 2.5
type N = 1 | 2

// ── ALL FOUR `needle: T` EXPORTS, UFCS, on the filed witness's receiver ──
function stdIndexOf(): i32 {
  const n: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const xs: Circle[][] = [n]
  return xs.indexOf(n)
}

function stdLastIndexOf(): i32 {
  const n: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const xs: Circle[][] = [n]
  return xs.lastIndexOf(n)
}

function stdIncludes(): boolean {
  const n: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const xs: Circle[][] = [n]
  return xs.includes(n)
}

function stdCount(): i32 {
  const n: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const xs: Circle[][] = [n]
  return xs.count(n)
}

// ── NO STRUCT ANYWHERE: the reviewer's disproof of the first axis ──
function mkM(i: i32): CM {
  const m: CM = Map()
  m["k"] = i
  return m
}
function noStruct(): i32 {
  const n = mkM(7)
  const xs: CM[] = [n]
  return xs.indexOf(n)
}

// ── the element rep with NO list core, and the one that trips exactly one gate ──
function noListCore(): i32 {
  const n: F[] = [1.5]
  const xs: F[][] = [n]
  return xs.indexOf(n)
}

// ── THE TWO-SENTENCE ROW, and the one carrying the full remedy clause. A NUMERIC literal
//    union's nullable is a value-union BOX — two cells with a tag — so it trips BOTH gates:
//    not structurally value-comparable, and no list core for the box list. Its STRING twin
//    `(K | null)[]` is the i32 atom-sentinel niche and RUNS (`silent-class-inventory` D101,
//    closed); the pair is this file's statement of where that boundary falls. Whoever makes
//    this row run must MOVE this directive rather than delete it; the paragraph above says
//    why, and it has now been moved twice. ──
function bothGates(): i32 {
  const n: (N | null)[] = [1]
  const xs: (N | null)[][] = [n]
  return xs.indexOf(n)
}

// ── OFF the std surface: a hand-written generic of the same signature, importing
//    nothing, so a reading of this file cannot conclude `std:array` chose the rule ──
function myIndexOf<T>(self: T[], needle: T): i32 {
  let i = 0
  while i < self.length {
    if self[i] == needle { return i }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return -1
}
function handWritten(): i32 {
  const n: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const xs: Circle[][] = [n]
  return myIndexOf(xs, n)
}

// ── THE CONTROL. The identical comparison, spelled out. Loud before this change and
//    loud after, in the SAME sentence the four generic routes above now use. ──
function control(): boolean {
  const a: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  const b: Circle[] = [{ r: 7 }]
  return a == b
}
